# Page Object and Component Object Core Rules

## Purpose

These rules define the ownership model for Page Objects and Component Objects.

The goal is to keep UI automation code simple, explicit, composable, and stable for AI-assisted development.

---

## Core Idea

Use Page Objects for pages, screens, routes, or navigation boundaries.

Use Component Objects for meaningful UI blocks inside pages.

A Page Object represents a page or screen and composes components.

A Component Object owns the behavior of a specific UI block.

Do not create abstractions "just in case".

---

## Default Approach

1. Start with a Page Object.
2. Keep simple page-specific locators and actions inside the Page Object.
3. Extract a utility only for pure reusable non-UI logic.
4. Extract a Component Object only when reuse, real complexity, semantic UI boundary, or Page Object growth justifies it.
5. Provide a Component Object through fixtures only if direct fixture access is clearly needed.

---

## Page Object Responsibility

A Page Object must represent one page, screen, route, or navigation boundary.

Page Objects may contain:

- page-level locators;
- user actions that belong to this page;
- navigation actions that belong to this page;
- page-loaded markers;
- simple helper methods for interactions on this page;
- composition of Component Objects.

Page Objects must not contain:

- `test.step`;
- test runner logic;
- hooks;
- test data generation;
- direct `process.env` access;
- unrelated navigation;
- cross-page business flows;
- speculative methods for future tests.

---

## Component Object Responsibility

A Component Object represents a meaningful UI block inside a page.

Component Objects may contain:

- component-level locators;
- user actions inside the component;
- helper methods for interacting with this component;
- simple state readers for this component;
- parsing logic directly related to component text or values.

Component Objects must not contain:

- `test.step`;
- test runner logic;
- hooks;
- test data generation;
- direct `process.env` access;
- unrelated page navigation;
- cross-page business flows;
- unrelated component behavior.

---

## Route and Navigation Boundary

Page Object ownership is primarily defined by route, screen, or navigation boundary.

Do not merge a page into another Page Object only because it visually looks related or is reachable from that page.

If a UI area has its own URL, screen state, or dedicated navigation entry, it should normally have its own Page Object.

Example:

```text
/products  -> ProductsPage
/login     -> LoginPage
/account   -> AccountPage
/checkout  -> CheckoutPage
```

Route or navigation ownership is stronger than visual placement.

When route ownership is unclear, use the UI Component Discovery rule before refactoring.

---

## Page Object as Composition Root

Page Objects should compose UI components instead of owning all UI logic.

Good structure:

```text
OwningPage
  ├── HeaderComponent
  ├── FilterPanelComponent
  ├── SortComponent
  └── ResultListComponent
```

Tests should normally access components through the owning Page Object.

Good:

```ts
await listPage.sorting.selectByValue("name-asc");

const names = await listPage.results.getNames();
```

Avoid exposing components directly as fixtures by default.

---

## No Cross-Page Business Flows

Do not hide cross-page workflows inside Page Objects or Component Objects.

Bad:

```ts
await checkoutPage.loginAndBuyItem();
```

Better:

```ts
await loginPage.login(user.email, user.password);
await catalogPage.results.openItem(itemName);
await cartPage.checkout();
await checkoutPage.placeOrder();
```

The test may orchestrate simple cross-page flows.

A dedicated domain or workflow layer may be introduced only when repeated cross-page business flows justify it.

---

## Method Design Rules

Methods in Page Objects and Component Objects should be:

- explicit;
- user-intent based;
- minimal;
- created only when required by current tests or current refactoring.

Good:

```ts
await listPage.sorting.selectByValue("price-desc");
```

Good:

```ts
const prices = await listPage.results.getPrices();
```

Bad:

```ts
await listPage.performAdvancedDynamicSortingWorkflowWithValidation();
```

Bad:

```ts
await listPage.clickElement("sort");
```

Avoid generic methods that hide intent.

---

## Public API Minimalism

Do not add public methods for future possible tests.

Only add methods required by current tests or current refactoring.

Bad:

```ts
async sortByRating(): Promise<void> {}

async sortByPopularity(): Promise<void> {}

async sortByNewest(): Promise<void> {}
```

Better:

```ts
async selectByValue(value: string): Promise<void> {
  await this.sortSelect.selectOption(value);
}
```

---
## Raw Locator Mechanics In UI Specs

UI specs must not contain raw selector mechanics for application UI elements.

Avoid direct usage in specs:

- page.locator("...");
- page.getByTestId("...");
- page.getByRole("...");
- page.getByText("...");
- page.getByLabel("...");

when a Page Object or Component Object should own the locator.

Specs should interact with application UI through:

- Page Object locators/actions/state readers;
- Component Object locators/actions/state readers, when component extraction is justified;
- dedicated assertion helpers, if applicable.

Allowed exceptions:

- Playwright-native page assertions that do not select application elements, for example URL assertions;
- browser/dialog/download/page-level APIs when they are the behavior under test;
- temporary investigation code that is not committed.

Example:

```ts
// Bad
await expect(page.locator("#email")).toBeVisible();

// Good
await expect(loginPage.emailInput).toBeVisible();
```
---

## Cross-Page Assertions

If a UI scenario navigates from one page to another, the spec must not assert destination page elements through:

- raw selectors;
- source Page Object;
- unrelated Component Object.

Use the destination Page Object for destination page markers.

Example:

Registration redirects to Login page.

Preferred:

- assert URL if relevant;
- assert LoginPage marker or LoginPage locator.

Avoid:

- asserting Login page elements with page.locator(...) inside the Registration spec;
- putting Login page locators into RegisterPage.

Do not make one Page Object own locators from another route, screen, or page.

## Main Principle

Page Object owns the page boundary.

Component Object owns a meaningful UI block.

Tests own the scenario.

Fixtures wire dependencies.

Domain flows, when justified, own reusable cross-page business workflows.


## Discovery Fallback

If Page Object or Component Object ownership is unclear, follow the UI Component Discovery rule before creating or moving abstractions.

Do not guess route ownership or component boundaries.
